{
  "id": "promontory_curtain_defense",
  "title": "Promontory Curtain Defence",
  "description": "An Edwardian Welsh defensive curtain closing off a promontory: a full stone circuit with round and D-shaped towers, a twin-tower gatehouse, arrow loops along every face, a deep approach ditch, and a compact keep with hall, chapel and kitchen ranges behind the curtain.",
  "style_id": "edwardian_welsh",
  "site_id": "m0a_promontory",
  "prompt": "You are fortifying a rocky promontory in Edwardian Wales. Close the neck with a battered curtain wall and full bailey circuit. Anchor the angles with round drum towers and at least one D-shaped tower facing the sea, and place a twin-tower gatehouse in the landward curtain. Pierce every curtain face with splayed arrow loops. Across the approach cut a deep ditch. Behind the curtain provide a compact keep, a great hall, a chapel and a kitchen range so the garrison can sustain a siege.",
